{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"ununbium-protective-silverfox","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Fleet notice primitive: TTL'd shared state for orchestrator-agent coordination\n\n## Problem (observed, 2026-08-11/13 bug-bash session)\nA ~30-agent, 3-session, 24-hour bug bash stress-tested rebar as a coordination substrate. Task-scoped communication worked well as-is: ticket comments carried premise corrections/evidence/dispositions durably; links carried provenance; claims were the load-bearing mutex (they arbitrated a two-session epic collision cleanly). But CROSS-CUTTING, fast-changing fleet state had no home:\n\n- The CI-failure playbook (stale-base signatures like `invalid choice: 'select'` and complexity-baseline drift → rebase; cert flakes → recheck; comment-hygiene → real fix) was discovered incrementally and had to be copy-pasted into ~30 agent briefs and re-sent point-to-point as it grew. The coordinator WAS the bulletin board — a single point of failure whose briefs bloated with boilerplate, and late-spawned agents missed anything omitted.\n- Transient environment state (\"main is RED on the complexity gate, hold pushes\", \"quiet window in effect, hold tracker transitions\", \"global install broken mid-update, use the worktree venv\") went out point-to-point and aged badly — one agent acted on a stale main-red note.\n- The absence of a well-known shared address caused a real anti-pattern: a work ticket (b902) accumulated cross-cutting analysis records as comments until the operator flagged it; records were migrated to session log d0d8-9dfe-f2d8-4364 (see its index).\n\n## Alternatives considered\n1. **Bulletin-board ticket by convention** — zero code, but formalizes the observed anti-pattern: unstructured append-only comment stream, no expiry, reads require scanning. Rejected.\n2. **Harness-side messaging improvements** — fixes delivery mechanics but is ephemeral (agent/session deaths were constant), unaudited, and invisible to non-Claude harnesses (against AGENTS.md's cross-vendor stance). Rejected as the primary mechanism.\n3. **Docs/AGENTS.md via normal Gerrit changes** — correct for STABLE lessons (several from this session should graduate: the playbook signatures, batch-plan-edits-then-review-LAST, the stale-tree grep trap, never-git-stash) but two review gates per update is wrong for state that changes in minutes. Complementary, not sufficient.\n4. **First-class notice primitive (PROPOSED)** — see below.\n\n## Proposal\nA small keyed notice surface in the tracker store (alongside, not inside, ticket dirs — precedent: bridge_alerts, .rebar/criteria_routing.json, env-scoped attestations are already store-level non-ticket shared state):\n\n- `rebar notice set <key> <text> [--ttl <duration>] [--scope fleet|session]` — upsert, records author env/session + timestamp; TTL default e.g. 24h; expiry is read-side (expired notices are omitted, history stays in the event log).\n- `rebar notice list [--all]` / `rebar notice clear <key>`.\n- **Surfacing is the key design point**: active notices are shown automatically at `claim` time and at `review-plan`/`verify-completion` start (count + text), so agents cannot miss them without ignoring their own command output. MCP read tool mirrors it.\n- Durable + auto-pushed like all tracker writes; write contention bounded by the landed lock-retry work (cc69cc1179).\n\n## Tradeoffs / risks\n- New surface to maintain; must not become config-by-another-name (mitigation: TTL mandatory, no code reads notices for behavior — display-only contract).\n- Hot-key write contention on busy fleets (mitigated by lock retries; notices are low-frequency writes).\n- Scope question: is fleet orchestration in a ticket system's remit? Adjacent prior art: idea ticket aurous-chewable-blackrhino (session-resumption contract). The display-only contract keeps this on the \"shared memo\" side of the line rather than orchestration logic.\n\n## Half-life rule (the framework this fits into, operator-endorsed direction)\nStable lessons → docs via normal changes. Task-scoped → ticket comments/links. Session records → session logs. Fast-changing fleet state → notices (this proposal).","id":"9d88-c56c-0855-4666","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Fleet notice primitive: TTL'd shared state surfaced at claim/review time"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786589064533493001,"uuid":"b2115674-989e-4463-a12a-b8ebec731fb2"}